mode: "100644"
text: "# The SHIFT framework — transformations that are credible, not just impressive\n\nBecause before/after is the most-faked format, the framework puts **credibility** at the center — an honest\ntransformation outperforms a suspiciously-perfect one. Each letter is a gate; most fail at H (endpoints only) or\nF (the disclosure).\n\n## S — Start with a real before\nAn honest, relatable, **un-staged** starting state — never exaggerate or stage a worse \"before\" for contrast (a\nmanufactured-worse before is deceptive). Capture it under the **same conditions** you'll use for the after. The\nbefore must be true, and ideally relatable enough that the viewer sees themselves in it.\n\n## H — Highlight the journey\n**The middle is the content.** Endpoints alone are a flex; the process — what actually changed it, the steps, the\n**setbacks**, and the **real timeline** — is what makes it credible, relatable, and valuable. Show the work.\n**Disclose the aids**: filters, medication (e.g. GLP-1), surgery, or professional help — *how* the result was\nachieved is material information, and hiding it is a deceptive omission.\n\n## I — Isolate the variables\nConsistent **angle, lighting, framing, distance, and conditions** so the change is genuinely the result — not a\nlighting/posing/edit trick. This is both craft and compliance: under the FTC's **net-impression test**, a visual\ntrick (a flattering after against an unflattering before) is itself a deceptive claim, even with zero words.\n\n## F — Frame results honestly (the disclosure gate)\n- **No cherry-picking / no survivorship** — don't show only your best case or only the people who stuck with it.\n- **If the result isn't typical, \"results not typical\" is NOT enough** — disclose the **actual typical/generally-\n  expected result** (use the **median**, not an outlier-skewed mean), **clearly and conspicuously** (close to the\n  claim, standing out, altering the net impression — not buried in hashtags), with **substantiation**.\n- **Use actual consumers** (or disclose they're not); **health claims** need competent, reliable scientific\n  evidence; **YMYL** gets not-professional-advice framing and no cure/guarantee. If you can't substantiate the\n  claim, don't imply the result is representative.\n\n## T — Tie it to the viewer\nMake the **before relatable** (they see themselves), the **after attainable** (honestly framed, real timeline),\nand give an **actionable takeaway** — what to actually do. A transformation that feels achievable and honest\nconverts; a flex that feels fake or unreachable doesn't. Then the agent specs the visual/script (routing to\n`short-form-video-script` / `carousel-writer`); the human/tool produces the real media; **WoopSocial publishes**.\n\n## Why honesty out-performs (not just protects)\nSkeptical audiences read a *too-perfect* before/after as a scam and scroll — so the visible signals of honesty\n(the same messy background, a disclosed setback, a real timeline, a \"this isn't typical\" line) don't just keep you\ncompliant, they *raise* trust and conversion. The believable transformation is the one people act on. Treat every\ndisclosure as a credibility feature, not a caveat you're forced to bury.\n"
